# The articles site reads no environment variables.
#
# There is nothing to copy to .env.local: `npm run dev`, `npm run build`
# and the Vercel build all run correctly with an empty environment. This
# file exists to say so in the place you would look, and to sit alongside
# class/.env.example so the pair reads as a decision rather than as one
# of them having been forgotten.
#
# Why there are none, and why that is structural rather than incidental:
# the site is a static export (`output: "export"`). It has no server, so
# it has nowhere to keep a secret — anything set here would be inlined
# into the bundle at build time and would therefore be public. Every
# secret in this repo lives in the class app precisely because it is the
# half that has a server
# (research/video-calls/07-two-app-architecture.md §1).

# CLASS_APP_DOMAIN was here, and is not any more.
#
# site/vercel.ts briefly built the /class rewrite destination from it, so
# that a preview of this site could point at a preview of the class app.
# Vercel validates that config *before* the build starts, and the one
# computed value in it resolved to nothing:
#
#   The `vercel.ts` schema validation failed with the following message:
#   `rewrites[0]` missing required property `destination`
#
# Every literal field in the same file validated fine. The deployment
# failed outright, production was rolled back to a commit predating the
# rewrite, and /class served a 404 until someone noticed. The destination
# is now written out in site/vercel.ts.
#
# Setting CLASS_APP_DOMAIN on the Vercel project does nothing today.
# Before wiring it back up, establish on a preview branch — where a
# failed build costs nothing — whether any environment value survives
# config validation at all. Vercel's `relatedProjects` is the built-in
# answer to the same problem and is the better thing to try first.

# Three things that look as though they belong here, and do not:
#
#   Vercel Analytics and Speed Insights (@vercel/analytics,
#   @vercel/speed-insights) are zero-config on Vercel. They read the
#   deployment's own identity; there is no key to supply.
#
#   Cover images are fetched by scripts/download-covers.mjs from URLs
#   recorded in scripts/cover-images.json. No image API is called, so
#   there is no image API key.
#
#   EL_WORKFLOW_ACTIVE is not a variable to set — it is named here only
#   so that finding it in the source does not send you looking for a
#   value. scripts/workflow.mjs sets it on the child processes it spawns
#   and refuses to start when it finds it already set, which is how it
#   catches an npm script that points back at the workflow runner.
#   Setting it by hand does nothing but break workflow:dev and
#   workflow:content.

# If a variable is ever added, it belongs here — and unless it is
# genuinely public, the fact that it needs to be secret is a sign it
# belongs in the class app instead.
